<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#yiddish">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yiddish</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yiddish is a language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi Jews</a>, based on medieval German with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and Slavic elements and written in the Hebrew alphabet. It became the everyday language of Jewish communities across central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> list Yiddish literary culture among the traditions of the Commonwealth's Jewish heartland that shaped global Jewish identity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yiddish.jpg" alt="Yiddish" width="320" height="127" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: joystick · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Machzor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Yiddish, historically called Judeo-German or Jewish German, is a West Germanic language that originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. By the 10th century a distinctive Jewish culture had formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>) and the Palatinate (notably Worms and Speyer), an area that came to be known as Ashkenaz. Yiddish gave the nascent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> community a vernacular based on High German, fused with many elements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, notably Mishnaic Hebrew, and to some extent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>. Most varieties also include Slavic elements. It has traditionally been written in the Hebrew alphabet, and its orthography developed towards the end of the High Middle Ages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The earliest surviving references, from the 12th century, call the language loshn-ashknaz, the language of Ashkenaz, or taytsh, a variant of the name for Middle High German. Colloquially it is mame-loshn, the mother tongue, as against loshn koydesh, the holy tongue, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>. The oldest surviving literary document in Yiddish is a blessing in the Worms machzor, a Hebrew prayer book, first recorded in 1272. Eastern Yiddish, far more common today, differs from Western Yiddish, which Ethnologue classifies as endangered, by its size and its extensive Slavic vocabulary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing</a> in the 16th century allowed cheap large-scale production of Yiddish works. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah-levita">Elia Levita</a>, the earliest named Yiddish author, composed the Bovo-Bukh about 1507-08, printed from 1541. From the late 18th century, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haskalah">Haskalah</a>, or Jewish Enlightenment, the language lost prestige, stigmatised by assimilationists and later by Zionists in favour of national languages and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>.</p><p>In the early 20th century, especially after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a>, Yiddish emerged as a major Eastern European language, with a flourishing literature, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and cinema, and the Yiddish Scientific Institute, YIVO, was founded in 1925. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> there were 11 to 13 million speakers. About 85 per cent of the approximately six million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> were Yiddish speakers, and the language declined massively. It is still spoken in Haredi communities, and a 2021 estimate counted 600,000 speakers in all.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yiddish” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dense network of Jewish communities in Polish and Lithuanian towns formed the demographic and cultural heartland of Ashkenazi Jewry, whose Talmudic academies, Hasidic movement and <b>Yiddish</b> culture shaped Jewish life worldwide for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Jewish communities have spoken many languages, among them Aramaic, Arabic, Judaeo-Spanish (Ladino) and <b>Yiddish</b>, the language of the Ashkenazim, while keeping Hebrew as the language of prayer and study; modern Hebrew was revived as a spoken language in the late 19th and early 20th centuries and is the…</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>…its sound system and syntax have been shaped by the languages of its first modern speakers, above all <b>Yiddish</b> and other European languages, and by Arabic: its standard pronunciation follows the Sephardi tradition, its word order is usually subject, verb and object, and it has a simple system…</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Its modern fame derives from Antoine Galland's French translation, which appeared from 1704 and was so successful that by the end of the century there were translations of it into English, German, Italian, Dutch, Danish, Russian, Flemish and <b>Yiddish</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>A possible source of the melody is a <b>Yiddish</b> tune, &quot;Koilen&quot;, recorded in New York in 1919 by the klezmer accordionist Mishka Ziganoff.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bella-ciao">Bella ciao</a></li><li><blockquote>It is also sung in <b>Yiddish</b>, Ladino, Judaeo-Italian and Judaeo-Arabic, and Haggadahs differ over whether the father bought or sold the goat.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chad-gadya">Chad Gadya</a></li></ul></section>
